WEBVTT

00:00:03.430 --> 00:00:04.553
It is ten past six.

00:00:05.355 --> 00:00:10.208
The site is quiet, and Dan, the site manager, still has today's diary to write.

00:00:11.088 --> 00:00:13.075
Most site diaries are written like this:

00:00:13.115 --> 00:00:15.865
at the end of a long day, from memory, in a hurry.

00:00:16.645 --> 00:00:19.053
Yet the diary is often the record that matters most

00:00:19.093 --> 00:00:21.722
when a delay or a variation is disputed months later.

00:00:22.802 --> 00:00:26.511
In this film, you will see how an AI tool can turn a short voice note

00:00:26.551 --> 00:00:28.496
into a structured diary entry,

00:00:28.536 --> 00:00:30.320
and what you still need to check before you sign it.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:34.768
By the end, you will be able to capture the day in a voice note,

00:00:34.808 --> 00:00:39.038
review an AI draft against what you saw, and search your records in plain English.

00:00:40.318 --> 00:00:44.209
Instead of facing a blank page, Dan records a voice note out on site.

00:00:45.011 --> 00:00:46.436
One minute, in his own words.

00:00:47.216 --> 00:00:51.619
Wednesday the twelfth. Dry and bright this morning, wind picked up after lunch.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.902
Crane stood down from one forty to ten past three.

00:00:55.702 --> 00:00:57.003
Fourteen on site:

00:00:57.043 --> 00:01:01.066
six bricklayers, four groundworkers, two scaffolders, me and Sarah.

00:01:01.867 --> 00:01:04.689
Ready-mix for the ground-floor slab was forty minutes late.

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:09.413
Pour finished about four. Scaffold inspection done on the north side.

00:01:10.173 --> 00:01:13.115
Revised window drawings for block B came in by email.

00:01:14.195 --> 00:01:18.262
The tool transcribes the note and sorts it into the company's diary template:

00:01:18.302 --> 00:01:22.349
weather, labour, plant, deliveries, progress,

00:01:22.389 --> 00:01:24.773
health and safety, and instructions received.

00:01:25.454 --> 00:01:29.170
The tool is told to use only what Dan said, and to add nothing he did not say.

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:34.721
It also flags gaps. Dan mentioned the crane stand-down, but not who made the call.

00:01:35.664 --> 00:01:37.428
The tool asks, rather than guesses.

00:01:38.408 --> 00:01:42.224
Then comes the most important step. Dan reads the draft against what he saw.

00:01:43.004 --> 00:01:44.146
The draft has two errors.

00:01:44.847 --> 00:01:48.332
It misread the crane times: Dan said one forty to ten past three.

00:01:49.193 --> 00:01:53.019
And it says the slab pour was completed, when only bay one of three was poured.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.343
Dan corrects both before he signs.

00:01:56.223 --> 00:02:00.051
The tool drafts. The site manager checks, corrects and signs.

00:02:00.873 --> 00:02:02.656
The signed entry is the record,

00:02:02.696 --> 00:02:05.001
and the responsibility stays with the person who signs it.

00:02:06.281 --> 00:02:09.577
Over weeks and months, the diaries become a record you can search.

00:02:10.357 --> 00:02:12.481
Dan asks, in plain English,

00:02:12.521 --> 00:02:15.006
how many hours the crane was stood down for wind in March.

00:02:15.887 --> 00:02:20.356
The answer links to each diary entry it came from, so every number can be checked.

00:02:21.636 --> 00:02:24.211
Before you start, agree three things with your company.

00:02:24.991 --> 00:02:29.288
Use an approved tool, so recordings and photos stay within your company's systems.

00:02:30.068 --> 00:02:31.994
Leave out personal details you do not need.

00:02:32.677 --> 00:02:35.265
A voice note that identifies people is personal data.

00:02:36.045 --> 00:02:38.213
And agree the diary template first,

00:02:38.253 --> 00:02:40.842
so every entry records the same facts in the same place.

00:02:42.122 --> 00:02:45.991
A quick check. The draft records the ready-mix as forty minutes late.

00:02:46.692 --> 00:02:50.260
The delivery ticket shows it was ninety minutes late. What do you do?

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:56.630
Correct the entry to match the ticket, and attach the ticket.

00:02:57.373 --> 00:02:58.657
You sign what the evidence shows.

00:02:59.937 --> 00:03:02.865
To recap: record the day while it is fresh,

00:03:02.905 --> 00:03:06.155
let the tool draft the entry, and check every fact before you sign.

00:03:07.235 --> 00:03:08.619
Next in the series:

00:03:08.659 --> 00:03:12.068
using AI to draft risk assessments and method statements,

00:03:12.108 --> 00:03:13.613
and where the competent person comes in.
